{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 2–4 €/t on average; larger at longer horizons).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the recent history turns sharply bearish, the model declines too slowly and keeps prices elevated. Increase sensitivity to recent negative momentum (steepen the forecast slope downward when last 5–10d returns are strongly negative).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: forecast error grows with lead time (later days are most overestimated). Use a horizon-scaled correction (small cut in days 1–5, larger cut in days 10–30).",
    "Insufficient drawdown risk: the model rarely projects the deeper sell-offs that occur in outcomes (tails). Add a downside stress adjustment when volatility rises and momentum is negative (e.g., widen lower band / subtract extra amount).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: model reverts upward too early after declines, while realizations continue drifting lower. Delay/attenuate upward mean reversion signals in bearish regimes (require stabilization in recent prices before reverting)."
  ]
}
